  "reasoning": "The patient's symptoms include a persistent dry cough, palpitations, recurrent infections that are not resolving, and intermittent throat pain. Step-by-step analysis points to bronchial asthma: 1) A chronic, non-resolving dry cough is a classic presentation of asthma (often cough-variant asthma). 2) Palpitations frequently accompany asthma due to transient hypoxia during airway constriction or as a known side effect of beta-agonist rescue inhalers. 3) Individuals with asthma have chronically inflamed airways, making them highly susceptible to recurrent respiratory infections that may linger longer than usual. 4) Intermittent throat pain is commonly caused by the mechanical irritation of frequent coughing or associated post-nasal drip. Other listed conditions are less likely: pneumonia typically involves a productive cough and fever; allergies usually present with sneezing, itchy/watery eyes, and rhinorrhea rather than palpitations or recurrent infections; common colds are acute and self-limiting; and cervical spondylosis focuses on musculoskeletal/neurological neck and back symptoms. Thus, bronchial asthma is the single best-fitting diagnosis.",
  "final_answer": "[DIAGNOSIS]bronchial asthma[/DIAGNOSIS]"
}
